                         "markdown":  "### OT Cyber Document Tool\n- The new tool is for documents that should look like the assessment and risk reports, such as proposals, method statements, reports, procedures and short notes.\n- A document with a cover prints with the same cover, document control page, header and footer as an assessment report. A short document has no cover and opens with its title.\n- Write with headings, lists, tables, pictures, footnotes, links, page breaks and landscape pages. The template sets fonts, sizes and colours.\n- Red prompts mark what is still to write. The Issue check lists them, and a Final export is refused while any remain.\n- Export a Word file as a Draft, with a watermark, or as a Final, which opens read-only.\n- Headings can be numbered, and a Contents list links to each heading.\n- The cover is always one page, and the footer carries the document\u0027s own classification.\n- A picture taller than a page, such as a phone screenshot, is fitted to the page.\n- Text pasted from Word keeps its footnotes. The tool lists anything it left out, such as comments, tracked deletions and pictures it could not read.\n\n### Bringing in a Word file\n- Load a `.docx` and it is rebuilt in the SolutionsPT template: headings, lists, tables, footnotes and links become the template\u0027s own.\n- Pictures are kept. Charts, SmartArt, shapes, text boxes, embedded sheets and equations are carried across as they were and print in the Word export, made smaller only where one would not fit the page.\n- A drawing that floated beside the text stays beside the same words, and a diagram drawn as loose shapes and lines is kept whole.\n- Anything that could not be carried over, such as comments, headers and footers, hidden text or a hand-made cover, is listed after the import, with a link to where it was. So is anything that was changed, such as list and heading numbers that no longer match a reference in the text.\n- The SolutionsPT cover and document control page of a report from these tools are recognised, used to fill in Document information and replaced by the current template\u0027s. The import report can put the originals back.\n- A first page that looks like another company\u0027s cover is pointed out on the import report, with a button to remove it.\n- The Word file you load is never changed.\n- Charts drawn as tables, such as a report\u0027s tiles, bars and heatmaps, keep their colours, widths and borders. Ordinary tables take the template\u0027s look.\n\n### Word preview in the assessment and risk tools\n- A new Word preview page shows the Word report page by page as it prints, with landscape pages, charts and coloured tables. It is drawn from the exported Word file.\n- In the assessment tool it follows the export\u0027s choices: Draft or Final, which site, zone or product, or the organisation-wide report. In the risk tool it shows the detailed or the high-level report.\n- You still edit the report on the report page, and the preview updates when it changes.\n\n### OT Cyber Risk Assessment Tool, redesigned\n- The tool follows the seven steps of IEC 62443-3-2:2020, from scope and risk criteria to approval. A Home page and the sidebar show the status of each step.\n- The three new steps are Risk comparison, Requirements specification and Approval. Risk comparison records where a detailed assessment is needed, and Approval can have more than one approver.\n- Every threat above tolerable risk needs a recorded treatment decision. The risk can be reduced with countermeasures, accepted, or treated through the SL-T programme. A suggestion is never reported as a decision.\n- Detailed risk is assessed for each zone separately. Each threat links to assets, vulnerabilities and countermeasures. It is rated with no countermeasures, with today\u0027s countermeasures and with the planned ones. A register, heat map and SL-T grid cover every zone.\n- Enter the client\u0027s own risk matrix, with impact and tolerable risk per consequence category.\n- Start from a library of 33 SolutionsPT threat scenarios, paste an asset register in from Excel, place zones in levels on the drawing, and undo the last change.\n- Compare with a previous assessment. The changes show on Check and issue, in the Word report and in Excel.\n- The Word report adds the drawing, risk register, treatment plan, accepted risks and risk criteria, and the cyber security requirements specification can be issued as its own document. Excel adds Summary, Treatment plan, Accepted risks, Vulnerabilities, Criteria, Assets and Changes sheets.\n- Risk project files from earlier versions open and are upgraded. Files saved by this version do not open in earlier versions of the risk tool.\n\n### Saving and files\n- The document tool saves to its own document file, with autosave and a warning before unsaved changes are lost.\n- A document file and a Word file both open with Load in the document tool.\n- Save as makes a separate document. Two windows of the tool can be open at once.\n\n### SolutionsPT OT Cyber Suite\n- The home page has a fourth card and the rail a fourth button, Write, for the document tool, with a tour of a demo proposal.\n- A document file dropped on the suite, or chosen with Open a file, opens in the document tool. Open Word files with Load in the document tool.\n- No files pass between the document tool and the other tools.\n- Close an open tool with the cross beside it in the rail or Close on its card. It opens fresh the next time. If it has unsaved changes, the suite asks first.\n\n### OT Cyber Report Review Tool\n- The report is shown as A4 pages, starting with the SolutionsPT cover, and reviewers can comment on the cover lines too.\n- In the suite, the buttons that passed a review pack straight to the review tool and the review file straight back are gone. The review is done by someone other than the author, so packs and review files are sent between people.\n\nSaved assessment project files from v0.5, v0.5.1 and v0.6 open unchanged."
